  "answer": "I couldn't see the error in your screenshot, so this match is a best guess. Similar past tickets report Command Palette failing to start because Windows could not register its MSIX package. Reinstalling PowerToys didn't help in those cases either. The usual causes were a policy blocking sideloading or app installation, or a deployment error such as 0x80070005 (access denied). To check, open the folder `C:\\Program Files\\PowerToys\\WinUI3Apps\\CmdPal`, or `%localappdata%\\PowerToys\\WinUI3Apps\\CmdPal` for a per-user install. Run the `.msix` file there and note the error it gives. You can also install it from an administrator PowerShell with `Add-AppxPackage -Path \"<path to the msix>\"`. Alternatively, quit PowerToys, restart it as administrator, and turn Command Palette off and on again. If sideloading is blocked, check Group Policy: 'Allow all trusted apps to install', 'Allow Sideloading of Apps' and 'Enable App Installer'. Also check Settings > Apps > Advanced app settings > Choose where to get apps. Then try installing again. If the error is something else, please post the exact error text, the PowerToys log lines about the CmdPal package registration, and the result of running the msix manually."
